# Script function and purpose: dinit service description. Declarative, and
# like the SysV script it launches Sakura directly rather than through a
# getty, so it resolves no terminal-handover program.
type            = process
restart         = true
smooth-recovery = true
# Action purpose: bind Sakura to the terminal the installer reserved, and let
# dinit's own setsid() make the shell a session leader, so that opening that
# terminal establishes it as Sakura's controlling terminal.
#
# The device has to be bound here because dinit has no per-service device
# setting: dinit-service(5) defines a console service's device as "the device
# to which dinit's standard output stream is connected", and inittab-line only
# "specifies the tty line that will be written to the \"utmp\" database". The
# shell is needed because dinit execs the command directly and does not
# interpret redirections itself.
#
# No setsid here, and no console option, both deliberately. dinit's
# src/run-child-proc.cc branches on on_console, which starts-on-console and
# shares-console both set:
#
#     if (!on_console) { ... setsid(); }   <- child becomes a SESSION leader
#     else { ...; setpgid(0,0); ... }      <- child becomes a GROUP leader
#
# So with shares-console the child leads its process group, and util-linux
# setsid(1) forks when its caller already does -- measured -- which would
# leave dinit supervising a parent that exits at once and, with restart = true,
# restarting it forever. With no console option dinit calls setsid() itself,
# the shell opens the terminal as session leader, and credentials(7) applies:
# "The controlling terminal is established when the session leader first opens
# a terminal (unless the O_NOCTTY flag is specified when calling open(2))."
# Measured that way: the pid is preserved, so dinit supervises Sakura itself,
# and the terminal is acquired.
#
# Dropping shares-console costs nothing here. It existed to share a console
# Sakura no longer takes, and a service with no console option neither queues
# for the console nor blocks services that hold it. dinit's non-console branch
# also points stdin at /dev/null and stdout at a logfile before exec; the
# redirection below replaces all three.
command         = /bin/sh -c "exec $PREFIX_DIRECTORY/bin/$EXECUTABLE_NAME </dev/tty$DEFAULT_TTY >/dev/tty$DEFAULT_TTY 2>&1"
depends-on      = login.target
termsignal      = HUP
